@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-long-stay-discount-10-off-total-at-3-days-rounded-to-nearest-cent
+type: decision-log
+title: "Long-stay discount: 10% off total at 3+ days, rounded to nearest cent"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+"A rental of 3 days or more now costs 10% less than its full price" leaves two things unsaid: how to
+round a 10% cut of an integer-cent price when it does not land on a whole cent, and where in the
+pipeline to apply the cut given `quote` and `book` must agree.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Where**: the discount is applied once, inside the shared `priceRequest` helper that both `quote`
+  and `book` already call, to the full price (`days * dailyPrice`). This keeps the two entry points
+  identical by construction instead of duplicating the rule.
+- **Threshold**: `days >= 3`, matching "3 days or more" literally; `countWholeDays` already guarantees
+  `days` is a whole number (dl-001), so there is no fractional-day boundary to worry about.
+- **Rounding**: `Math.round(fullPrice * 0.9)`, i.e. round-half-away-from-zero to the nearest cent.
+  Money is always a non-negative integer here, so this is equivalent to round-half-up; no currency
+  library or alternative rounding mode (floor/ceil) was introduced, consistent with the "no added
+  tooling" stance from dl-001.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A rental's total can no longer be computed as `days * dailyPrice` for stays of 3+ days; callers must
+  go through `quote`/`book`, which they already do.
+- Rounding means the discount is not always exactly 10% of the full price (off by at most half a cent);
+  this is accepted as standard cent-rounding behaviour rather than a defect.
+- If a future request asks for a different discount shape (e.g. tiered by length, or per-day instead of
+  on the total), `applyLongStayDiscount` is the single place to change.
